## Native objects — ECMAScript standard

```js
// Available in every JS environment
new Array(3);         // native
new Map();            // native
Promise.resolve(1);   // native
JSON.stringify({});   // native
```

Their behaviour is fully specified — `Array.prototype.map` works identically in Chrome, Firefox, Node, Deno, Bun.

## Host objects — environment provided

```js
// Browser only:
document.querySelector('#app');
window.localStorage.getItem('key');
fetch('/api/data');

// Node.js only:
process.env.PORT;
Buffer.from('hello', 'utf8');
```

## Why the distinction matters

- Code that only uses native objects is portable across environments (browser ↔ Node).
- Code that uses host objects must either be environment-specific or polyfilled (e.g., a `fetch` polyfill for Node 17 and earlier).
- Extending or monkey-patching host objects is even riskier than extending native ones — the host environment may change the implementation without notice.

## The grey area: globals

`console`, `setTimeout`, `clearTimeout`, and `fetch` started as host objects in browsers but are now also available in Node.js and Deno — they have become de-facto standards even without ECMAScript specification.

